1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,275
Welcome to ComPort Zone, a terminal and
automation workbench for Windows.

2
00:00:06,495 --> 00:00:13,120
In this tutorial, we will identify a device,
save useful commands, run a repeatable

3
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:16,393
check, and build a live monitoring panel.

4
00:00:16,613 --> 00:00:20,965
Everything shown uses a local demonstration
instrument.

5
00:00:21,185 --> 00:00:27,372
You can follow along without a physical board,
a serial adapter, or a COM port.

6
00:00:29,017 --> 00:00:34,905
Download the Windows installer or portable zip
from the project's releases page.

7
00:00:35,125 --> 00:00:41,226
Run the installer, or extract the whole
portable folder and open the application.

8
00:00:41,446 --> 00:00:45,030
The packaged desktop app includes its own
runtime.

9
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:50,838
If you are working from source, run the setup
development batch file, followed by

10
00:00:50,838 --> 00:00:52,631
the launch app batch file.

11
00:00:52,851 --> 00:00:56,905
The illustrated manual covers both
installation paths.

12
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:03,891
The tutorial device is a small Python script
in the tools, media folder.

13
00:01:04,111 --> 00:01:08,825
Open PowerShell in the repository and run the
command shown here.

14
00:01:09,045 --> 00:01:12,096
It uses only Python's standard library.

15
00:01:12,316 --> 00:01:15,985
Keep that terminal open while following the
tutorial.

16
00:01:16,205 --> 00:01:20,984
The server listens on your own computer, on
port fifty twenty five.

17
00:01:21,204 --> 00:01:26,132
Its voltage, current, temperature, and status
readings are simulated.

18
00:01:27,767 --> 00:01:33,465
The center of the window is a workspace for
terminal, editor, and Control Panel

19
00:01:33,465 --> 00:01:33,825
tabs.

20
00:01:34,045 --> 00:01:39,961
The drawer on the left keeps favorites, saved
commands, files, and history close at

21
00:01:39,961 --> 00:01:40,317
hand.

22
00:01:40,537 --> 00:01:46,383
In a terminal, outgoing and incoming messages
use separate rows and colors.

23
00:01:46,603 --> 00:01:50,037
The editable line begins with the transmit
prompt.

24
00:01:50,257 --> 00:01:55,036
The connection indicator and footer help you
confirm the current target.

25
00:01:56,683 --> 00:01:59,734
Open Connection Settings for your terminal
tab.

26
00:01:59,954 --> 00:02:06,581
Choose TCP, enter the localhost address shown
here, set the port to fifty twenty

27
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:10,557
five, and select carriage return plus line
feed.

28
00:02:10,777 --> 00:02:12,889
Accept the settings and connect.

29
00:02:13,109 --> 00:02:17,247
A green connection indicator means the socket
is open.

30
00:02:17,467 --> 00:02:22,247
These settings belong to this tab, so another
terminal can use a different

31
00:02:22,247 --> 00:02:24,443
connection and a different device.

32
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:32,083
Click after the transmit prompt, type the
identification query shown on screen, and

33
00:02:32,083 --> 00:02:32,948
press Enter.

34
00:02:33,168 --> 00:02:36,475
The demo replies with its name and serial
number.

35
00:02:36,695 --> 00:02:41,921
You have now checked the complete path from
the application to the target and back.

36
00:02:42,141 --> 00:02:45,917
Device commands depend on your instrument or
firmware.

37
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:52,022
ComPort Zone sends them; it does not require
every device to understand this

38
00:02:52,022 --> 00:02:54,500
particular identification query.

39
00:02:56,183 --> 00:03:00,130
Try the voltage, current, temperature, and
status queries.

40
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:06,547
At the default settings, voltage is near
twelve volts, current is near zero point

41
00:03:06,547 --> 00:03:09,225
four two amps, and status is ready.

42
00:03:09,445 --> 00:03:12,709
Small changes between readings are
intentional.

43
00:03:12,929 --> 00:03:19,070
Notice how timestamps and message direction
make it easy to associate a query with

44
00:03:19,070 --> 00:03:19,819
its reply.

45
00:03:20,039 --> 00:03:24,925
Keep this terminal connected for the
automation and monitoring examples.

46
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:32,360
For a physical serial device, create another
terminal and select Serial instead.

47
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:38,868
Pick its COM port and match the baud rate,
data bits, parity, stop bits, and flow

48
00:03:38,868 --> 00:03:41,817
control to the firmware documentation.

49
00:03:42,037 --> 00:03:44,064
Set the required line ending.

50
00:03:44,284 --> 00:03:50,300
DTR and RTS can affect the board, so choose
their states intentionally.

51
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:57,618
The local tutorial demonstrates traffic over
TCP; this serial settings screen is a

52
00:03:57,618 --> 00:03:59,523
configuration example.

53
00:04:01,183 --> 00:04:04,362
TCP provides a connected byte stream.

54
00:04:04,582 --> 00:04:10,449
UDP sends separate datagrams and does not
establish a remote connection.

55
00:04:10,669 --> 00:04:17,397
For UDP, a green indicator means the local
socket is available, not that a device

56
00:04:17,397 --> 00:04:18,477
has answered.

57
00:04:18,697 --> 00:04:24,834
The tutorial server supports both on the same
port, so you can try a UDP terminal

58
00:04:24,834 --> 00:04:25,289
later.

59
00:04:25,509 --> 00:04:32,698
Serial and TCP support auto reconnect; UDP has
no connection to reestablish.

60
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:38,745
Send the voltage setting command, followed by
the voltage query.

61
00:04:38,965 --> 00:04:42,997
The write is silent; the query confirms the
new setting.

62
00:04:43,217 --> 00:04:45,905
This is common for instrument protocols.

63
00:04:46,125 --> 00:04:50,413
A missing reply to a write is not
automatically an error.

64
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:55,774
Verify changes using the device's documented
readback command.

65
00:04:55,994 --> 00:05:02,353
Reset the demonstration instrument before the
next exercise so its output is enabled

66
00:05:02,353 --> 00:05:05,381
and its voltage returns to twelve volts.

67
00:05:07,017 --> 00:05:10,985
Use the up and down arrows to recall earlier
commands.

68
00:05:11,205 --> 00:05:16,261
Control plus Space offers completions from
history and saved commands.

69
00:05:16,481 --> 00:05:21,387
The History page provides another way to find
and reuse what you sent.

70
00:05:21,607 --> 00:05:24,381
Control plus F searches the terminal.

71
00:05:24,601 --> 00:05:30,264
You can adjust text size with Control and the
mouse wheel, and toggle timestamps or

72
00:05:30,264 --> 00:05:32,515
line wrapping from the View menu.

73
00:05:34,183 --> 00:05:37,234
Text mode adds your selected line ending.

74
00:05:37,454 --> 00:05:43,702
Hex Bytes mode sends exactly the bytes
entered, so include any required terminator

75
00:05:43,702 --> 00:05:44,387
yourself.

76
00:05:44,607 --> 00:05:50,620
The bytes shown here spell ping followed by
carriage return and line feed; the demo

77
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:51,562
answers pong.

78
00:05:51,782 --> 00:05:56,625
Receive display mode only changes how incoming
bytes appear.

79
00:05:56,845 --> 00:05:59,085
It does not change the bytes sent.

80
00:05:59,305 --> 00:06:03,081
Return to text mode for the remaining
exercises.

81
00:06:04,767 --> 00:06:08,905
Use terminal logging when you need a record of
communication.

82
00:06:09,125 --> 00:06:14,947
Select a file, confirm that logging is on, and
stop logging when the capture is

83
00:06:14,947 --> 00:06:15,611
complete.

84
00:06:15,831 --> 00:06:20,801
Receive pause holds the display; it does not
disconnect your device.

85
00:06:21,021 --> 00:06:24,541
Script pause controls command-file progress
instead.

86
00:06:24,761 --> 00:06:31,037
Later, Control Panel CSV logging will record
parsed measurements, which is useful

87
00:06:31,037 --> 00:06:33,593
for a different kind of analysis.

88
00:06:35,267 --> 00:06:40,387
Open Saved Commands in the drawer and add a
command with a useful label.

89
00:06:40,607 --> 00:06:47,071
For example, save the identification query as
Identify in a Tutorial group.

90
00:06:47,291 --> 00:06:50,832
Star frequently used commands to put them in
Favorites.

91
00:06:51,052 --> 00:06:55,468
Keep each command's text or hexadecimal send
mode correct.

92
00:06:55,688 --> 00:07:01,852
Before sending from a library, check the
active terminal so a useful shortcut goes

93
00:07:01,852 --> 00:07:03,581
to the intended device.

94
00:07:05,267 --> 00:07:09,875
The tutorial includes a command library in CSV
format.

95
00:07:10,095 --> 00:07:16,101
Import it through the saved-command import
action, reviewing the append or replace

96
00:07:16,101 --> 00:07:16,687
options.

97
00:07:16,907 --> 00:07:20,747
Export a CSV when sharing your own library.

98
00:07:20,967 --> 00:07:26,920
The Files page stores paths to command files,
and those entries can be favorites

99
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:27,217
too.

100
00:07:27,437 --> 00:07:33,365
A file shortcut does not include the file's
contents, so copy the actual scripts

101
00:07:33,365 --> 00:07:35,736
when moving to another computer.

102
00:07:37,433 --> 00:07:42,468
Open the first check command file from the
examples, tutorial folder.

103
00:07:42,688 --> 00:07:46,912
The editor shows line numbers and highlights
the script language.

104
00:07:47,132 --> 00:07:51,463
Choose your connected tutorial terminal as the
run target.

105
00:07:51,683 --> 00:07:54,605
This matters when several sessions are open.

106
00:07:54,825 --> 00:08:00,675
The prepared script identifies the device,
repeats voltage measurements, checks the

107
00:08:00,675 --> 00:08:02,719
result, and writes a summary.

108
00:08:02,939 --> 00:08:07,099
Inspect a shared script before running it
against your equipment.

109
00:08:08,767 --> 00:08:12,799
Double braces mark a parameter supplied before
the run.

110
00:08:13,019 --> 00:08:15,067
Count has a default of three.

111
00:08:15,287 --> 00:08:20,748
A dollar-prefixed name is a runtime variable,
created while the script runs.

112
00:08:20,968 --> 00:08:26,685
Capture extracts a value from the device reply
using a regular expression.

113
00:08:26,905 --> 00:08:29,252
Wait delays use milliseconds.

114
00:08:29,472 --> 00:08:35,598
The response timeout also uses milliseconds,
so fifteen hundred means one and a half

115
00:08:35,598 --> 00:08:38,880
seconds, rather than fifteen hundred seconds.

116
00:08:40,517 --> 00:08:43,994
Run the script and keep the count parameter at
three.

117
00:08:44,214 --> 00:08:49,876
With the demo at its defaults, you should see
three passing checks and a completion

118
00:08:49,876 --> 00:08:50,422
summary.

119
00:08:50,642 --> 00:08:56,298
The loop repeats the measurement, capture
saves its numeric value, and the condition

120
00:08:56,298 --> 00:08:58,386
compares it with the threshold.

121
00:08:58,606 --> 00:09:04,089
Log messages explain the result without
sending their text to the device.

122
00:09:04,309 --> 00:09:08,575
Read the run status as well as the individual
replies.

123
00:09:10,267 --> 00:09:13,957
Pause and Resume control a running command
file.

124
00:09:14,177 --> 00:09:15,756
Stop ends the run.

125
00:09:15,976 --> 00:09:21,331
A Pause instruction creates an operator
checkpoint, while Break leaves a loop.

126
00:09:21,551 --> 00:09:25,583
By default, a response check that times out
stops the script.

127
00:09:25,803 --> 00:09:29,899
Increase the timeout only when the device
needs more time.

128
00:09:30,119 --> 00:09:34,791
The continue-on-error setting changes this
behavior deliberately.

129
00:09:35,011 --> 00:09:39,299
The manual links to the complete scripting
reference for these options.

130
00:09:40,933 --> 00:09:45,371
Open the Control Panel manager with Control,
Shift, and D.

131
00:09:45,591 --> 00:09:50,455
Import the tutorial panel JSON and open
Tutorial Bench.

132
00:09:50,675 --> 00:09:53,725
Bind it to the connected tutorial terminal.

133
00:09:53,945 --> 00:09:59,236
The panel shares that terminal's connection
instead of opening a competing one.

134
00:09:59,456 --> 00:10:02,165
Confirm the target in its binding chip.

135
00:10:02,385 --> 00:10:08,727
The prepared panel includes measurements, a
status indicator, calculated power, an

136
00:10:08,727 --> 00:10:12,284
output toggle, and a numeric voltage setpoint.

137
00:10:13,933 --> 00:10:19,926
Each measurement tile sends a query on its own
schedule, collects the response, and

138
00:10:19,926 --> 00:10:21,443
applies a parse rule.

139
00:10:21,663 --> 00:10:27,721
The voltage tile reads the first reply line as
a number and adds the volts unit.

140
00:10:27,941 --> 00:10:33,595
For replies containing labels, use a regular
expression to extract the value.

141
00:10:33,815 --> 00:10:37,612
Choose intervals and timeouts appropriate to
your hardware.

142
00:10:37,832 --> 00:10:43,295
Poll traffic stays out of the normal terminal
transcript, keeping manual work

143
00:10:43,295 --> 00:10:43,933
readable.

144
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:49,632
Color rules turn a reply into an
understandable state.

145
00:10:49,852 --> 00:10:54,801
The tutorial temperature tile reports failure
above fifty degrees.

146
00:10:55,021 --> 00:11:01,251
Rules are evaluated in order, so place
specific conditions before broad ones.

147
00:11:01,471 --> 00:11:06,015
The power tile combines voltage and current
with an expression.

148
00:11:06,235 --> 00:11:12,229
It calculates a value from other tiles rather
than sending another device command.

149
00:11:12,449 --> 00:11:17,569
Clear labels make these references easier to
understand and maintain.

150
00:11:19,267 --> 00:11:23,811
Numeric tiles can show a sparkline beneath the
current value.

151
00:11:24,031 --> 00:11:27,060
Double-click one to open its larger chart.

152
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:31,803
Select a time span and use the hover readout
to inspect points.

153
00:11:32,023 --> 00:11:36,737
A newly opened panel needs time to gather a
useful history.

154
00:11:36,957 --> 00:11:39,432
This chart is for live inspection.

155
00:11:39,652 --> 00:11:46,210
Enable CSV logging when you need measurement
records that remain available after the

156
00:11:46,210 --> 00:11:47,225
session ends.

157
00:11:48,850 --> 00:11:51,709
Send the demo fault command from the terminal.

158
00:11:51,929 --> 00:11:57,454
Status changes to fault and temperature rises
above the configured threshold.

159
00:11:57,674 --> 00:12:03,498
The panel records transitions into failure or
error states in its alert history.

160
00:12:03,718 --> 00:12:07,515
You can control alert sounds per tile or
globally.

161
00:12:07,735 --> 00:12:10,210
Send the fault-off command to recover.

162
00:12:10,430 --> 00:12:17,406
Acknowledging or silencing an alert changes
the notification; it does not repair or

163
00:12:17,406 --> 00:12:18,835
reset the device.

164
00:12:20,517 --> 00:12:22,799
Writing tiles start disarmed.

165
00:12:23,019 --> 00:12:29,315
Enable Master Arm before using the output
toggle or voltage setpoint, and press

166
00:12:29,315 --> 00:12:31,467
Escape to disarm afterward.

167
00:12:31,687 --> 00:12:35,207
Readback should confirm the device's actual
state.

168
00:12:35,427 --> 00:12:41,111
Disarming blocks further panel writes; it does
not undo a setting or send an

169
00:12:41,111 --> 00:12:42,531
output-off command.

170
00:12:42,751 --> 00:12:48,965
Master Arm applies to panel controls, while
terminal and script sends retain their

171
00:12:48,965 --> 00:12:50,026
own workflows.

172
00:12:51,683 --> 00:12:57,145
Choose a file and enable CSV logging to record
parsed values.

173
00:12:57,365 --> 00:13:03,701
The kind column distinguishes polled values,
calculated values, and control sends.

174
00:13:03,921 --> 00:13:09,211
Keep the application open and the PC awake
during an unattended run.

175
00:13:09,431 --> 00:13:15,227
Polling normally pauses when the target
disconnects or a command file uses its

176
00:13:15,227 --> 00:13:16,045
connection.

177
00:13:16,265 --> 00:13:22,414
Check the binding chip to understand a pause,
and verify each target when using a

178
00:13:22,414 --> 00:13:24,691
panel across multiple devices.

179
00:13:26,350 --> 00:13:31,022
Split the workspace to keep the command-file
editor beside its terminal.

180
00:13:31,242 --> 00:13:37,571
You can also compare two devices, then join
panes when the extra view is no longer

181
00:13:37,571 --> 00:13:38,111
useful.

182
00:13:38,331 --> 00:13:40,721
Give tabs recognizable names.

183
00:13:40,941 --> 00:13:46,713
The command palette opens with Control, Shift,
and P and helps you find actions

184
00:13:46,713 --> 00:13:47,298
quickly.

185
00:13:47,518 --> 00:13:52,972
Preferences and the View menu provide themes,
terminal fonts, wrapping, and

186
00:13:52,972 --> 00:13:54,281
timestamp options.

187
00:13:55,933 --> 00:14:01,864
The command-line interface uses the same
application settings and scripting engine.

188
00:14:02,084 --> 00:14:04,324
Send runs a one-shot query.

189
00:14:04,544 --> 00:14:09,024
Validate checks a command file without
contacting a device.

190
00:14:09,244 --> 00:14:15,531
Run executes the saved check, and parameter
flags provide values for unattended

191
00:14:15,531 --> 00:14:16,327
operation.

192
00:14:16,547 --> 00:14:21,176
These commands are shown against the same
local tutorial server.

193
00:14:21,396 --> 00:14:27,241
The manual explains how to activate the source
environment if the command is not on

194
00:14:27,241 --> 00:14:27,945
your path.

195
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:35,536
An automation script should check the process
exit code, not just look for familiar

196
00:14:35,536 --> 00:14:35,893
text.

197
00:14:36,113 --> 00:14:41,468
Zero indicates success, while eleven indicates
a failed response check.

198
00:14:41,688 --> 00:14:48,109
Supply required parameters and use
noninteractive operation in unattended jobs.

199
00:14:48,329 --> 00:14:55,071
Avoid opening a physical COM port from the GUI
and CLI at the same time.

200
00:14:55,291 --> 00:15:01,691
The CLI reference lists all commands,
connection flags, and stable exit codes.

201
00:15:03,350 --> 00:15:10,534
Export application settings as JSON, saved
commands as CSV, and Control Panels

202
00:15:10,534 --> 00:15:12,929
through their JSON export.

203
00:15:13,149 --> 00:15:17,906
Share command files themselves, together with
a description of the target.

204
00:15:18,126 --> 00:15:22,158
A quick-file export shares paths, not script
contents.

205
00:15:22,378 --> 00:15:27,327
App-settings transfer intentionally keeps
quick-action libraries separate.

206
00:15:27,547 --> 00:15:33,622
Import a panel, bind the correct terminal, and
verify commands and ranges before

207
00:15:33,622 --> 00:15:36,507
adapting a tutorial to real equipment.

208
00:15:38,183 --> 00:15:44,053
If a device does not reply, check its address,
command, line ending, and connection

209
00:15:44,053 --> 00:15:44,690
settings.

210
00:15:44,910 --> 00:15:50,073
Garbled serial text usually calls for checking
line configuration.

211
00:15:50,293 --> 00:15:53,962
A busy port may belong to another tab or
program.

212
00:15:54,182 --> 00:15:58,598
For a script failure, inspect the actual
response and timeout.

213
00:15:58,818 --> 00:16:02,722
For a stopped panel, check its binding and
pause reason.

214
00:16:02,942 --> 00:16:07,849
The manual includes a symptom table with the
next action for each case.

215
00:16:09,517 --> 00:16:15,523
You have now used a terminal, built a reusable
command library, run a repeatable

216
00:16:15,523 --> 00:16:19,351
check, and monitored a device with a Control
Panel.

217
00:16:19,571 --> 00:16:26,124
Start your own workflow with one known query,
verify the reply, and add automation a

218
00:16:26,124 --> 00:16:27,294
step at a time.

219
00:16:27,514 --> 00:16:34,409
The user manual, scripting guide, CLI
reference, and tutorial examples are linked

220
00:16:34,409 --> 00:16:36,111
from the repository.

221
00:16:36,331 --> 00:16:39,979
Stop the demo server with Control C when you
finish.
